That's not the way I read it.  The codes says:

    if (u.ulevel > 2 && u.uluck >=3D 0 &&
        !rn2(10 + (2 * u.ugifts * nartifacts))) {
            /* try to generate a gift */
    }

So you must be at least experience level 3 (but beyond that,
experience has no effect) and have non-negative luck.  After
that, it's 1 / (10 + 2*gifts*artifacts); the number of artifacts
in existance doesn't affect your chances for the first gift
(which is 1 in 10), but it does for all remaining gifts.

If I'm a legal, I'll generally go for Excalibur first, and not
bother sacrificing for gifts beyond that.  If you're restricted
in long sword---a Valk isn't---, it may be worth sacrificing
until you get a long sword gift, to unrestrict it.  And if
you're not opposed to wishing for artifacts, it might be worth
intentionally avoiding any gifts, so that you can successfully
wish for Magicbane later.  (In practice, I generally end up with
a gift or two even when I'm only sacrificing in order to know if
it's safe to pray.  Which means that by the time I've got the
castle wand, it's too late to wish for artifacts.  And I've got
some worthless junk---Grayswandir is the only artifact I'd use
instead of Excalibur.  And even then...)

